TLSH hashsets
=============

Hash type SFHASH_TLSH (1 << 15), field name "tlsh", hash length 36.

Rows
----

Each HDAT row is the 36-byte canonical TLSH digest described in
include/hasher/tlsh.h:

   0 L          log-scaled input length
   1 Q          (Q1 << 4) | Q2
   2 plane_hi   16 bytes; bit i = bit 1 of bucket code i
  18 plane_lo   16 bytes; bit i = bit 0 of bucket code i
  34 checksum
  35 reserved   always 0

The layout is chosen so that the bytewise sort applied to every HDAT is the
canonical TLSH order (L, Q1, Q2, body, checksum). Rows are sorted but not
unique: when records are written, two records with the same digest and
different other fields produce two identical rows, each mapped to its own
record by the RIDX entry at the same index. (A hashsets-only file has no
RIDX and its rows are deduplicated, like every other hash type.)

The null digest ("TNULL", all 36 bytes zero) never appears as a row. In the
text input a TLSH column of "TNULL" is treated like an empty column: the
record is written with the field marked absent and contributes no row.

HINT type 0x7400: cell index
----------------------------

A TLSH hashset's HINT chunk has hint type 0x7400 (big-endian uint16, like
the 0x6208 block hint) followed by

  uint64_t offset[65537]   little-endian

The cell of a digest is its first two bytes, c = (L << 8) | Q, so a cell is
a contiguous run of rows in the sorted HDAT. The rows of cell c are
[offset[c], offset[c+1]); offset[0] == 0, offset[65536] == hash count, and
offset is non-decreasing (an empty cell has offset[c] == offset[c+1]).

The hint data starts 12 bytes into the chunk and the offsets 14 bytes in,
so readers must not assume the offset array is 8-byte aligned.

A lookup binary-searches the digest's cell. Nearest-neighbour search
(sfhash_hashset_tlsh_knn) scans all rows unless the hashset has a TIVF
index (below).

Optional chunks: TVEC and TIVF
------------------------------

A TLSH hashset section may be followed by two optional chunks, in this
order, each belonging to the section whose HDAT precedes it:

  HHnn HINT HDAT [RIDX] [TVEC] [TIVF]

Neither may appear in a section of any other hash type (a reader rejects
the file). Both are written by default for TLSH fields when hashsets are
written; sfhash_hashset_builder_set_tlsh_options (or HSET_TLSH_OPTIONS for
mkhashset) turns either off. The FTOC reserves room for both whether or not
they are written, so the FTOC chunk may be shorter than the gap before
FHDR.

TVEC ("TVEC", 0x54564543): the lower-bound Euclidean embedding of every
row, see docs/TLSH_EMBEDDING.md sections 1-2. Payload:

   0 version   uint16_t, 1
   2 variant   uint8_t, 0 (128 buckets, 1-byte checksum)
   3 flags     uint8_t, 0
   4 dims      uint32_t, 264
   8 n         uint64_t, equals the section's hash count
  16 dtype     uint32_t, 1 = float32 little-endian
  20 reserved  uint32_t, 0
  24 reserved  uint64_t, 0
  32 data      float32[n][dims], row r is the embedding of HDAT row r

The chunk is placed so that data starts on a 4096-byte boundary of the
file: offset(TVEC) + 12 + 32 is a multiple of 4096, with zero padding
before the chunk as needed. The matrix can therefore be memory-mapped and
handed directly to numpy, faiss, etc.; sfhash_hashset_tlsh_vectors returns
a pointer to it.

TIVF ("TIVF", 0x54495646): an inverted-file index over the body embedding
for exact pruned kNN, see docs/TLSH_EMBEDDING.md sections 3-4. Lists are
grouped by header cell (the same (L, Q) cells as HINT 0x7400); each cell
is split by k-means over the body embedding into lists of about 32 rows.
Payload:

   0 version     uint16_t, 3
   2 flags       uint16_t, 0
   4 dims        uint32_t, 256 (body embedding dimensions)
   8 lists       uint32_t, C
  12 reserved    uint32_t, 0
  16 n           uint64_t, equals the section's hash count
  24 reserved    uint64_t, 0
  32 cell_lists  uint32_t[65537], lists of cell c are [cell_lists[c], cell_lists[c+1])
     medoids     36-byte TLSH rows [C], one digest per list
     radius2     uint32_t[C], every member of the list is within this
                 squared body-embedding distance of the medoid
     list_start  uint64_t[C+1], CSR offsets into ids
     ids         uint32_t[n], HDAT row indexes, ascending within a list

The medoid of a list is any digest such that every member is within radius2
of it; the search only relies on that bound. A freshly built index uses a
member (the one nearest the k-means centroid) with the exact radius, but a
merged index (below) may keep a medoid whose row is no longer a member, and
a radius that is merely an upper bound. Lists are never empty, and the lists
of a cell partition the cell's rows; in particular when a cell has a single
list its ids are the cell's row range.

A reader checks the version, dims, length, row count, cell and list offsets;
the ids are not validated at load time. The TIVF payload is only 4-byte
aligned in general, so readers must not assume list_start is 8-byte
aligned.


Set operations
==============

sfhash_hashset_merge (and the hsetop tool) computes the union, intersection
or difference (left minus right) of two hset files by merging, not by
rebuilding: every HDAT is the two-pointer merge of the inputs' sorted rows,
and each derived structure is carried through that merge by the row map
(old row -> new row, or dropped) in O(n) with no sort, no re-embedding and
no re-clustering. The builder-based sfhash_hashset_builder_*_open functions
remain and rebuild everything from the record tables.

Hash sections. The output has a section for every hash type present in
either input (left's types in left's order, then right's extras), with the
left input's field name. A type on one side only is merged against an
empty run: union and difference copy it, intersection empties it.

Records. When both inputs have record tables the output has one: records
are widened to the union of the fields (left's fields, then right's
extras; absent fields are a zero presence byte and zero value), merged in
sorted order, and identical records collapse to one (the left copy). A
side whose field order changed by the widening is re-sorted first. Rows
then follow their records: a row is emitted iff its record is, equal rows
belonging to different records stay duplicates (as the builder writes
them), and RIDX is remapped through the record map. When only one input
has records, or neither, the output is a hashsets-only file: no RHDR, RDAT
or RIDX, and equal rows (within or across the inputs) are written once. A
records-only input (RDAT but no hash sections) can only be merged with
another records-only input, giving a records-only output.

Cell index (HINT 0x7400) is recomputed from the merged rows as a prefix
sum.

TVEC rows are copied from the input that contributed the row; a row from
an input without vectors is embedded with tlsh_embed. TIVF lists are
carried through merge_tlsh_ivf: per cell, the left input's lists followed
by the right's, ids remapped, dropped ids removed, lists that become empty
omitted, medoids and radii unchanged. The members of an output list are a
subset of the input list's, so the stored radius remains a valid bound and
the indexed search stays exact. If either input lacks a TIVF and the output
wants one, build_tlsh_ivf clusters the merged rows from scratch instead.

Options. tlsh_options selects the optional TLSH sections of the output as
for the builder (SFHASH_HSET_TLSH_VECTORS, SFHASH_HSET_TLSH_INDEX), or
SFHASH_HSET_TLSH_INHERIT (hsetop's default) writes each section iff every
input that has a TLSH section has it. Repeated merges only shrink lists
(union appends lists, intersection and difference thin them), so after
many merges a from-scratch build tightens the index; it never becomes
incorrect.

Layout is as for a built file: HDAT chunks start on a 4096-byte boundary,
TVEC data is 4096-aligned, the FTOC lists exactly the chunks written, and
the SHA-256 covers the file from offset 40.

sfhash_hashset_tlsh_knn uses the TIVF index when present and brute force
otherwise; both are exact for every max_distance. sfhash_hashset_tlsh_knn_with
selects a strategy explicitly.
